Database Replication

1. What is Data Replication?
	keeping copies of the same data in multiple locations and synchronizing changes between them.
	Why? High availability, Disaster recovery, Read scalablity, Lower latency.
	
2. Replication vs Backup
	| Replication                       | Backup                       |
	| --------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |
	| Continuous/current copies         | Point-in-time snapshot       |
	| Used for availability/failover    | Used for recovery            |
	| Replica can take over quickly     | Restore takes time           |
	| May replicate corruption/deletion | Can restore older good state |
	
3. How Replication Works
	Initial snapshot -> Capture changes -> Send changes -> Apply changes to replica
	Changes can be captured using - Transaction logs, Database triggers, CDC (Change Data Capture)
	CDC - it reads database change logs and captures INSERT UPDATE DELETE.
	It is commonly used because it can capture changes with relatively low impact on the primary and preserve transaction order.
	
4. Synchronous Replication
	The primary waits for the replica to confirm the write.
	it has strong consistency between primary and replica and lower chances of losing recently committed data.
	But it has higher latency and dependent on replica/network availability
	
5. Asynchronous Replication
	Primary acknowledges the write without waiting for replicas.
	it has lower write latency and better for geographically distant replicas.
	But there can be replication lags. like some changes on primary and if primary fails during this lag, some recently written data may not yet exist on the replica.
	
6. Replication Lag
	difference between the primary's latest state and a replica's state.
	This creates the classic problem, write -> primary and read from replica, Result may be stale.
	
7. Transactional Replication
	Changes are replicated in transaction order.
	usually take an initial snapshot, send subsequent changes, replica applies them incrementally.
	
8. Snapshot Replication
	Copies the database state at a particular point in time.
	
9. Merge Replication
	Multiple nodes can independently accept changes. Useful when nodes need to operate independently, including offline scenarios.
	Problem - two nodes may modify the same data.
	
10. Key-Based Replication
	Uses a replication key such as: updated_at, incrementing_id
	Only records changed since the previous replication are copied.
	it's efficient and puts relatively little load  on the source.
	Problem - Deletes can be missed, because the deleted row no longer exists to be discovered through the key.
	
11. Full vs Partial Replication
	Full - Every replica contains the entire dataset. simple reads, complete copy but more storage, bandwidth and replication cost.
	Partial - Replica contains only a subset. Less storage and bandwidth but more complicated routing and queries.
	
12. Benefits of Replication
	High Availability, Read Scaling, Lower Latency.
	
13. RPO vs RTO
	RPO - Recovery Point Objective, How much data can we afford to lose?
	RTO - Recovery Time Objective, How long can the system be unavailable?
	
