Database types

1. Relational Database (RDBMS)
	Data is stored in tables with rows and columns, with relationships through keys.
	Good for - structed data, Relationships and joins, ACID transactions, Financial systems, Enterprise applications.
	
2. Key-Value Database
	store data as "user:123" -> {name : "Alice"}
	Good for - Caching, Sessions, Shopping carts
	e.g - Redis, DynamoDB.
	
3. Document Database
	Stores semi-structured documents, usually JSON/BSON.
	good for - flexible schemas, Product catalogs, CMS
	e.g - mongodb, CouchBase.
	
4. Graph Database
	stores node + edge + Properties
	Good for - social networks, Recommendation systems.
	e.g - amazon neptune.
	
5. Wide-Column Database
	Data is distributed across many machines and organized around flexible columns.
	good for - Huge datasets, High write throughput, Real time analytics.
	e.g - Cassandra.
	
6. In-Memory Database
	Stores data primarily in RAM instead of relying on disk for every access.
	good for - caching, Gaming state, Real time applications.
	e.g - redis, memcached.
	
7. Time-Series Database
	Designed for time-stamped data.
	good for - monitoring, metrics, IOT.
	e.g - InfluxDB, TimescaleDB.

8. Object-Oriented Database
	Stores data as objects, similar to how applications using OOP languages represent data.
	Good for - Complex object models, OOP-heavy applications.
	e.g - ObjectDB.
	
9. Text Search Database
	Optimized for searching large amounts of text.
	support things like - Full-text search, Ranking, Filtering.
	Good for - Product search, Log search.
	e.g - Elastic Search.
	
10. Spatial Database
	Designed for geographical and geometric data.
	Good for - Maps, Nearby searches, Route planning.
	e.g - Oracle Spatial.
	
11. Blob / Object Datastore
	Stores large unstructured objects: images, videos, audio, pdfs.
	good for - media storage, backups.
	e.g - Amazon s3.

12. Ledger Database
	Designed for immutable, append-only records.
	Useful when you need a verifiable history of changes.
	Good for - Financial records, Supply chains, Audit trails.
	e.g - Amazon QLDB.
	
13. Hierarchical Database
	Stores data as a tree.
	Each record has one parent and potentially many children.
	good for - Hierarchial structures, File systems
	e.g - IBM IMS
	
14. Vector Database
	Stores high-dimensional vectors, usually embeddings.
	Used for similarity / nearest-neighbor search.
	good for - AI/ML applications, Semantic search, Recommendation systems.
	e.g - Pinecone, FAISS
	
15. Embedded Database
	Runs inside the application process instead of as a separate database server.
	good for - Desktop applications, Mobile/edge applications, Games.
	e.g - SQLite, RocksDB.
	
